## DESCRIPTION
##   Estimating directional derivatives
## ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject(Calculus - multivariable)
## DBchapter(Differentiation of multivariable functions)
## DBsection(Directional derivatives and the gradient)
## Date(10/15/09)
## Institution(Fort Lewis College)
## Author(Paul Pearson)
## MLT(MaxRateOfChangeApplication)
## Level(4)
## MO(1)
## TitleText1('Calculus')
## AuthorText1('Hughes-Hallett')
## EditionText1('4')
## Section1('14.4')
## Problem1('')
## TitleText2('Calculus')
## AuthorText2('Hughes-Hallett')
## EditionText2('5')
## Section2('14.4')
## Problem2('')
## KEYWORDS('Gradient', 'Directional Derivative')

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros(
    'PGstandard.pl',        'PGML.pl',
    'parserVectorUtils.pl', 'parserRadioButtons.pl',
    'contextFraction.pl',   'PGcourse.pl'
);

Context("Vector");

#  function
$a = random(100, 200, 10);
$b = random(1,   4);
$f = Formula("$a / ( x^2 + y^2 + $b )");

#  Part A
$rb = RadioButtons(
    [
        [ 'circles', 'ellipses', 'hyperbolas', 'lines', 'parabolas' ],
        'None of the above'
    ],
    0,
    labels => 'ABC'
);

#  Part B
$criticalpt = Point(0, 0);
$maxtemp    = Real("$a/$b");

#  Part C
#  point
($x, $y) = (non_zero_random(-3, 3, 1), non_zero_random(-3, 3, 1));

#  gradient
$fx = $f->D('x');
$fy = $f->D('y');

$gradf   = Formula("< $fx, $fy >")->reduce;
$gradfab = $gradf->eval(x => $x, y => $y)->reduce;

BEGIN_PGML
The temperature at any point in the plane is
given by [`` T(x,y) = [$f] ``].

a) What shape are the level curves of [`T`]?'

    [_]{$rb}

b)  At what point on the plane is it hottest? [_]{$criticalpt}

c) What is the maximum temperature? [_]{$maxtemp}

d) Find the direction of the greatest increase in temperature at the point [` ([$x],[$y]) `].  [_]{$gradfab->cmp(parallel=>1, sameDirection=>1)}

e) What is the value of this maximum rate of change, that is, the maximum value of the directional derivative at [` ([$x],[$y]) `]? [_]{norm($gradfab) -> cmp}

f) Find the direction of the greatest decrease in temperature at the point [` ([$x],[$y]) `]. [_]{(-1 * $gradfab)->cmp(parallel=>1, sameDirection=>1)}

g) What is the value of this most negative rate of change, that is, the minimum value of the directional derivative at [` ([$x],[$y]) `]?
[_]{-1 * norm($gradfab)}
END_PGML

$s = (norm($gradfab))**2;
Context("Fraction");
$ab = Compute("$a/$b");

$f1 = Compute("-2*$a*$x/($x*$x+$y*$y+$b)^2");
$f2 = Compute("-2*$a*$y/($x*$x+$y*$y+$b)^2");

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
a) Let [` k >0`] be constant. The level curves have equation 
[` k =[$f] `] or [` x^2 + y^2 = \frac{[$a]}{k} - [$b] `]. If  
[` \frac{[$a]}{k} - [$b] > 0 `], these equations represent circles in the
[`xy`]- plane.

b) The largest value of the temperature [`T`] is attained at [`(0,0)`]. 

c) The value of the temperature at this point is [`T(0,0) = \frac{[$a]}{[$b]}=[$ab] `]

d)  The  greatest increase in temperature at [` ([$x],[$y])`] is in the direction of the gradient at the point.

    We have [` \nabla f (x,y) = [$gradf] \; \Rightarrow \nabla f([$x], [$y]) =  \left\langle [$f1], [$f2]\right \rangle \approx [$gradfab] `].  

e) The maximum value of the directional derivative at [` ([$x], [$y]) `] is given by the magnitude of the gradient at [` ([$x], [$y]) `]: [` \; \left| \nabla f([$x], [$y]) \right| =  \sqrt{[$s]} `]

f)  The greatest decrease in temperature at [` ([$x],[$y])`] is in the opposite
direction of the gradient at the point: [`` -\nabla f([$x], [$y]) = - [$gradfab] ``].

g) The minimum value of the directional derivative at [` ([$x], [$y]) `] is
    given by [` -\left| \nabla f([$x], [$y]) \right| =  -\sqrt{[$s]} `]

END_PGML_SOLUTION
ENDDOCUMENT();
